.TH PIDOF 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
pidof \- list the process IDs of running programs by name
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pidof
.RB [ \-s ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR pid ]...\&
.RI [ name ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pidof
writes the process ID of each running process whose name is one of the
.I name
operands.
It takes the operands in order, and for each one writes the IDs of the
processes with that name.
The IDs go on one line of standard output, separated by spaces.
When no ID is written, nothing is written, not even a newline, and the
exit status is 1.
It reads no input.
Its messages go to standard error.
.PP
This
.B pidof
is a busybox applet; see
.BR busybox (1).
A
.I name
is an exact process name, not a pattern.
To select processes by pattern, use
.BR pgrep (1).
.PP
The IDs for one name are in the reverse of the order in which Windows
lists the processes, which need not be sorted by ID.
A process that matches several operands is written once for each, so
.B pidof sh sh
writes every shell twice.
The list of processes is read again for each operand, and covers every
process on the system, including those of other users.
.PP
The process running
.B pidof
is named
.BR pidof ,
so
.B pidof pidof
writes its own ID.
.SS Process names
Only the name of a process is matched, never its path or command line.
The name is the file name of its executable as Windows reports it,
including the extension, such as
.BR explorer.exe .
The comparison is case-sensitive, although Windows file names are not,
and the extension is part of the name, so
.B make
does not match
.BR make.exe .
An operand with a directory part, such as
.BR /bin/sh ,
matches nothing.
.PP
A process running the same build of busybox is named after its applet
instead, such as
.B sh
or
.BR sleep .
A script whose
.B #!\&
line names an applet is named after the script file, except that a shell
script run from the kit's shell is named after the shell, such as
.BR sh .
The kit's command launchers, such as
.B sleep.exe
and
.BR pidof.exe ,
appear under their own names, each alongside the busybox process it
started, so
.B pidof sleep
finds the busybox process and
.B pidof sleep.exe
finds the launcher.
A busybox process of a different build, or one that
.B pidof
cannot open, is named after its executable file instead, such as
.BR busybox.exe .
.PP
Only the first 15 bytes of a long name are compared.
A process name of 15 or more bytes matches any operand that agrees with
it in its first 15 bytes, however the two differ after that.
So
.B pidof x86_64\-w64\-mingw32\-gcc.exe
also finds
.BR x86_64\-w64\-mingw32\-g++.exe ,
and the extension may be left off a name with 15 or more bytes before
it.
A shorter process name must match the whole operand.
.PP
The idle process, with process ID 0 and the name
.BR "[System\ Process]" ,
is never written.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-so ,
and the argument of
.B \-o
may be attached, as in
.BR \-o4 .
Options may follow the names.
.B \-\-
ends the options, so that every later argument is a
.IR name ,
even one that begins with a dash.
.TP
.B \-s
Write only one ID for each name: the first that would otherwise be
written, after any omitted by
.BR \-o .
The limit is per name operand, not for the whole command, so
.B pidof \-s sh sleep
writes up to two IDs.
.TP
.BI "\-o " pid
Omit the process ID
.I pid
from the output.
The option may be repeated, to omit several IDs.
.I pid
is a decimal integer from 0 to 4294967295, or
.BR %PPID ,
which stands for the ID of the parent of
.BR pidof ,
the process that started it; see
.BR NOTES .
A parent that has exited has ID 1, unless Windows has since reused its
ID.
.IP
A value that is neither, including a number with a sign or a
.B 0x
prefix, fails with
.B invalid number
and exit status 1.
This happens only when
.B pidof
compares the value to an ID it found, so a bad value goes unnoticed if
nothing is found, or if earlier
.B \-o
values omit every ID found.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit with status 0.
It is recognized only as the first argument.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the operands are expanded as file names before
.B pidof
sees them, even inside quotes, as needed when run from
.BR cmd.exe .
A name is never a pattern, so the effect is only that an operand such as
.B app*
is replaced by the names of matching files in the current directory,
which are then taken as process names.
A pattern that matches nothing is left as it is.
A busybox process started with the variable unset sets it to
.B 0
for its children, so commands run from the kit's shell receive their
operands as the shell expanded them.
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
When it is set, even to an empty value, the C library's option parser
stops at the first operand, so that
.B \-s
and
.B \-o
are recognized only before the first
.IR name .
.PP
Variables read by the startup code shared by all applets, such as
.B BB_CRITICAL_ERROR_DIALOGS
and
.BR BB_UMASK ,
do not change what
.B pidof
does; see
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
At least one process ID was written, or
.B \-\-help
was given.
.TP
.B 1
No process ID was written: no process matched, every match was omitted
by
.BR \-o ,
or there was no
.IR name .
Also an invalid option, a
.B \-o
with no argument, or an invalid
.B \-o
value.
.PP
An invalid option or a missing argument is reported on standard error,
followed by the usage message.
An invalid
.B \-o
value is reported on standard error as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
pidof: invalid number \(aq\fIvalue\fR\(aq
.EE
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
Find a running program:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ sleep 60 &
[1] 22068
$ pidof sleep
22068
.EE
.RE
.PP
Test whether a program is running:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ pidof gdb.exe >/dev/null && echo running
.EE
.RE
.PP
Count the running instances of a program, as the IDs are all on one
line:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ pidof sleep | wc \-w
3
.EE
.RE
.PP
Write the IDs one per line:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ pidof sleep | tr \(aq \(aq \(aq\en\(aq
.EE
.RE
.PP
List the other shells, leaving out the shell that runs the command:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ pidof \-o %PPID sh
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from the procps
.BR pidof ,
and from busybox on Linux:
.IP \(bu 2
Only a process's name is matched.
Busybox on Linux also matches the name a program was started with, and
the name of its executable file, or its full path when the operand
begins with a slash.
.IP \(bu 2
Names include the extension and are case-sensitive.
.IP \(bu 2
There are no options other than
.B \-s
and
.BR \-o .
.PP
The list of processes is a snapshot, so a process may have exited by the
time its ID is written.
The applet name of a busybox process is read from the memory of that
process, which fails for a process that Windows does not let busybox
open, such as an elevated one or one of another user.
Names are compared as bytes in the ANSI code page, which is UTF-8 on
Windows 10 version 1903 or later; see
.BR busybox (1).
.PP
Unlike
.BR kill (1)
and
.BR killall (1),
which the kit's shell runs without starting a process,
.B pidof
runs in a process of its own, whose parent is the shell.
As the last command of
.BR "sh \-c" ,
or under
.BR exec ,
it runs in the shell's own process instead, so its parent, and so
.BR %PPID ,
is the parent of that shell, and that shell, now named
.BR pidof ,
is not found as
.BR sh .
When started through the kit's
.B pidof.exe
launcher, as from
.BR cmd.exe ,
the parent is the launcher instead, not the program that ran the
launcher.
Then
.B %PPID
is the ID of the launcher, and omits only a process named
.BR pidof.exe .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR kill (1),
.BR killall (1),
.BR pgrep (1),
.BR pkill (1),
.BR ps (1),
.BR sh (1)
